            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Consider a prime $p$ of the form $4 t+3$. Show that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ iff $-a$ has order $(p-1) / 2$.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"number_theory\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Let \\\\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\\\), which is odd.\\n\\nFirst suppose that \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\), so \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\\\). Then \\\\(a^m\\\\not\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), while \\\\((a^m)^2\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\(p\\\\) is odd, this implies \\\\(a^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Hence\\n\\\\[\\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\\\equiv(-1)(-1)\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nIf \\\\((-a)^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), then \\\\(a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\(a\\\\) has order \\\\(2m\\\\), we have \\\\(2m\\\\mid2n\\\\), and therefore \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Thus \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\\\).\\n\\nConversely, suppose that \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\\\). Put \\\\(x=-a\\\\), so \\\\(x\\\\) has the odd order \\\\(m\\\\) and \\\\(a=-x\\\\). Certainly\\n\\\\[\\na^{2m}=(-x)^{2m}=x^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nNow suppose that \\\\(a^n=(-x)^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). If \\\\(n\\\\) were odd, then \\\\(x^n\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Squaring would give \\\\(x^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), so \\\\(m\\\\mid2n\\\\). Because \\\\(m\\\\) is odd, \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\), which would imply \\\\(x^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), a contradiction since \\\\(p\\\\) is odd. Therefore \\\\(n\\\\) is even. Then \\\\(x^n=a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), so \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Since \\\\(m\\\\) is odd and \\\\(n\\\\) is even, \\\\(2m\\\\mid n\\\\). Consequently \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m=p-1\\\\), and hence \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\).\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
